# ============================================================

## Project Overview
Specification Clarification Application.
Workflow-based AI system that converts Minutes of Meeting (MOM)
into structured specification documents with ambiguity/risk detection.
Not a chat AI. AI is only called in controlled workflow steps.
2 developers. Pre-production / university project + talent competition.

## Tech Stack
- Backend:     NestJS (TypeScript), ports & adapters architecture
- Database:    PostgreSQL 16, TypeORM
- Queue:       AWS SQS (async AI generation)
- AI:          OpenAI API direct (gpt-4o-mini)
- Storage:     AWS S3 (private bucket)
- Secrets:     AWS Secrets Manager (injected by ECS at runtime)
- Frontend:    Vercel (Next.js) — separate repo
- Mobile:      Review-only app — separate repo
- CI/CD:       GitHub Actions → ECR → ECS Fargate

## Environment Convention
- NODE_ENV=local        → developer machine, Docker PG, no SSL
- NODE_ENV=development  → shared dev server, RDS dev, SSL enabled
- NODE_ENV=staging      → pre-prod, mirrors production config
- NODE_ENV=production   → live, RDS prod, SSL enabled
- SSL enabled when NODE_ENV !== 'local'
- AWS credentials: explicit only when present (local dev)
  on ECS task role handles auth automatically — never require credentials

## Project Structure
backend/
  src/
    main.ts                          → HTTP API entry point
    worker.main.ts                   → SQS worker entry point (no HTTP)
    app.module.ts                    → root module
    constant/
      sql-column.constant.ts         → column type constants
    entities/
      base.entity.ts                 → base entity (id + isActive + audit columns)
      *.entity.ts                    → one file per entity
    modules/
      user/
      template/
      template-section/              → flat structure, same level as template
      spec/
      queue/                         → SQS producer (used by API)
      worker/                        → SQS consumer (used by worker)

## Entities
All entities extend BaseEntity from backend/src/entities/base.entity.ts
BaseEntity contains:
- id: UUID primary key (@PrimaryGeneratedColumn('uuid'))
- isActive: boolean, default true
- createdBy: UUID FK → user.id, nullable
- createdOn: timestamptz, default NOW()
- updatedBy: UUID FK → user.id, nullable
- updatedOn: timestamptz, default NOW()
- deletedBy: UUID FK → user.id, nullable
- deletedOn: timestamptz, nullable

Never repeat audit columns on individual entities.
Never add id column on individual entities.
Never add isActive on individual entities — inherited from BaseEntity.
Always import column types from backend/src/constant/sql-column.constant.ts.
No magic strings for column types anywhere.

AuditLogEntity does NOT extend BaseEntity — it has its own columns only.

Entity files: backend/src/entities/
- base.entity.ts
- user.entity.ts
- language.entity.ts
- spec-status.entity.ts
- risk-type.entity.ts
- main-template.entity.ts
- template.entity.ts
- template-section.entity.ts
- main-generated-spec.entity.ts
- generated-spec.entity.ts
- generated-spec-section.entity.ts
- spec-risk.entity.ts
- audit-log.entity.ts

NOTE: section-type.entity.ts does NOT exist — section_type table was dropped.
template_section uses title + description for AI guidance instead.

## Entity: Language
- code: varchar(10), unique, not null — used by frontend/API (values: 'EN', 'TH')
- name: varchar(50), unique, not null — display name (values: 'English', 'Thai')
Frontend always sends code ('EN' or 'TH'), backend resolves to id internally.
Zod validation: z.enum(['EN', 'TH']) for language input.

## Entity: SpecStatus
- code: varchar(20), unique, not null — used by app code
- name: varchar(50), unique, not null — display name
Values: PROCESSING | COMPLETED | FAILED | REVIEWED
NOTE: PENDING status was removed — specs start with PROCESSING immediately
Use SpecStatusCode enum, never magic strings.

## Entity: RiskType
- code: varchar(50), unique, not null — used by app/AI
- name: varchar(100), unique, not null — display name
Values: AMBIGUOUS_LANGUAGE | MISSING_OWNER | NO_TIMELINE | ASSUMED_FACT | UNCLEAR_SCOPE
Use RiskTypeCode enum, never magic strings.

## Entity: MainTemplate
- languageId: UUID FK → language.id, not null
- currentVersion: UUID FK → template.id, nullable
- Same pointer pattern as MainGeneratedSpec.currentVersion
- Only updated when a template version is finalized
- Future-proofed for team sharing feature
- Never updated on draft/failed template versions
- Language is fixed at template root level — cannot change after creation
- Language determines spec output language (no separate language selection on create/regen)

## Entity: TemplateSection
- title: varchar(255), not null
- description: TEXT, nullable — used as AI guidance (tells AI what to generate)
- sortOrder: int, not null, default 0
- NO sectionTypeId — section_type table was dropped entirely

## Entity: MainGeneratedSpec
- name: varchar(255), not null — stable across all versions
- description: TEXT, nullable — optional user-provided description, editable after creation
- currentVersion: UUID FK → generated_spec.id, nullable — latest COMPLETED version
- pendingVersion: UUID FK → generated_spec.id, nullable — set on create/regen start, cleared on COMPLETED or FAILED

## Entity: GeneratedSpec
- version: int, not null, default 1
- momInputType: varchar(10), not null, default 'TEXT' — 'TEXT' | 'FILE'
- momS3Key: varchar(500), nullable
- momS3Bucket: varchar(255), nullable
- aiModel, promptTokens, completionTokens, totalTokens, generationTimeMs — AI metadata
- exportedOn: timestamptz, nullable — updated on PDF export, no EXPORTED status
- templateVersionId: UUID FK → template.id — exact version used at generation time

## Entity: SpecRisk
- priority: varchar(10), not null — 'HIGH' | 'MEDIUM' | 'LOW' — AI-determined
- sectionId: nullable FK → generated_spec_section.id

## Entity: AuditLog (does NOT extend BaseEntity)
- id: UUID PK
- entity: varchar(100) — entity name e.g. 'GeneratedSpec'
- entityId: UUID — no FK constraint intentionally
- action: varchar(20) — 'CREATE' | 'UPDATE' | 'DELETE'
- changedFields: jsonb, nullable
- changedBy: UUID FK → user.id, nullable (null for worker operations)
- changedAt: timestamptz

## Enums
export enum SpecStatusCode {
  PROCESSING = 'PROCESSING',
  COMPLETED = 'COMPLETED',
  FAILED = 'FAILED',
  REVIEWED = 'REVIEWED',
}

export enum RiskTypeCode {
  AMBIGUOUS_LANGUAGE = 'AMBIGUOUS_LANGUAGE',
  MISSING_OWNER = 'MISSING_OWNER',
  NO_TIMELINE = 'NO_TIMELINE',
  ASSUMED_FACT = 'ASSUMED_FACT',
  UNCLEAR_SCOPE = 'UNCLEAR_SCOPE',
}

export enum MomInputType {
  TEXT = 'TEXT',
  FILE = 'FILE',
}

export enum RiskPriority {
  HIGH = 'HIGH',
  MEDIUM = 'MEDIUM',
  LOW = 'LOW',
}

## TypeORM Rules
- synchronize: false always, no exceptions
- No eager: true on any relation
- Always use @JoinColumn({ name: 'snake_case_column' }) on @ManyToOne side
- camelCase property names, TypeORM maps to snake_case DB columns automatically
- nullable FK columns use { nullable: true } in both @Column and @ManyToOne
- Relations loaded explicitly via relations: {} in queries, never auto-loaded
- All relations define both sides: @ManyToOne on child, @OneToMany on parent
- No @Exclude() decorators on entities
- namingStrategy: SnakeNamingStrategy from typeorm-naming-strategies
- Always filter isActive: true + deletedOn: IsNull() on active record queries
- SSL: enabled when NODE_ENV !== 'local' — { rejectUnauthorized: false }

## AWS SDK Credentials Pattern
- Never require AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID / AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY in config validation
- Make credentials optional in all AWS config services (get not getOrThrow)
- Only pass credentials to SDK clients when explicitly present:
  if (accessKeyId && secretAccessKey) { clientConfig.credentials = {...} }
- On ECS: task role provides credentials automatically via instance metadata
- On local: credentials from .env used when present
- Never store AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID / AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY in Secrets Manager

## Transaction Management
- Use @nestjs-cls/transactional + @nestjs-cls/transactional-adapter-typeorm
- @Transactional() decorator on service methods that span multiple repos
- ClsPluginTransactional registered in both AppModule and WorkerModule
- Worker wraps handleMessage in cls.runWith({}) — no HTTP middleware in worker
- No manual manager passing to repositories
- FAILED status update always outside transaction — must persist even on rollback

## NestJS Config Pattern
- ConfigModule.forRoot() in AppModule only — loads .env locally, no-op on ECS
- ConfigModule.forFeature(registerAs('domain', () => ({...}))) per domain module
- Each domain has its own ConfigService wrapper class
- No USE_AWS_SECRET flag
- No AWS SDK calls at bootstrap
- All config reads from process.env — source does not matter to the app
- ECS injects Secrets Manager values into process.env before container starts

registerAs domains:
- 'database'  → DB_HOST, DB_PORT, DB_USER, DB_PASS, DB_NAME
- 'openai'    → GEN_AI_API_KEY, GEN_AI_MODEL
- 'storage'   → STORAGE_PROVIDER, AWS_S3_BUCKET, AWS_S3_REGION
- 'queue'     → SQS_QUEUE_URL, SQS_REGION
- 'jwt'       → JWT_ACCESS_TOKEN_SECRET, JWT_REFRESH_TOKEN_SECRET,
                JWT_ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRES_IN, JWT_REFRESH_TOKEN_EXPIRES_IN

## DTO Pattern
- Zod schemas for all DTOs — inferred types, NOT classes
- DTOs are plain TypeScript types inferred from Zod schemas
- Use plain mapper functions (toXxxDto) when mapping entity to response
- Do NOT use static DTO.from() — DTOs are not classes
- Controller owns response shape — maps entity to DTO
- Exception: auth methods (login, refresh) — service returns DTO directly
- Methods shared with SQS worker return entity, not DTO

## Service / Controller Pattern
- Never return raw TypeORM entities from controllers
- Service returns entity/domain object by default
- Controller calls mapper function to shape response
- TypeORM relations loaded explicitly only when needed
- No @Exclude() needed — mapper functions handle response shaping

## Layering Rules
- Controller → validates HTTP, calls service, maps to DTO
- Service → business logic, orchestrates repositories
- Repository → data access only, no business logic
- Repository never resolves status names to IDs — service does that
- Repository never knows WHY it's doing something — only HOW

## Repository Pattern
- Custom repository class per entity wrapping TypeORM Repository<T>
- Repository owned by its feature module via forFeature([Entity])
- Never share repository across modules — share service instead
- DatabaseModule handles TypeORM connection only, no repository providers
- Feature modules export their Service, never their Repository
- Select only needed columns in list queries
- Always filter deletedOn: IsNull() + isActive: true on active record queries
- Auth queries (findByEmailWithPassword) explicitly select password field
- All other queries never select password

## Spec List Behavior
- List page shows current version only (main_generated_spec with currentVersion)
- Falls back to pendingVersion for status when currentVersion is NULL (first gen)
- isRegenerating = true only when BOTH currentVersion AND pendingVersion exist
- Previous versions accessible via Versions tab on detail page
- Never list all versions on the list page
- Always scoped to current authenticated user (userId filter)

## pendingVersion Pattern
- Set on BOTH first-time spec creation AND regeneration
- Cleared on COMPLETED (worker success) and FAILED (worker failure)
- currentVersion NULL + pendingVersion set = first generation in progress
- currentVersion set + pendingVersion set = regeneration in progress
- currentVersion set + pendingVersion NULL = stable, no active generation
- Never update currentVersion until worker COMPLETED
- isRegenerating flag in list response = !!currentVersion && !!pendingVersion

## PDF Export
- Uses pdfkit
- Synchronous — user waits, returns buffer directly
- No S3 storage of exported PDFs
- On export: update generated_spec.exported_on = new Date()
- No EXPORTED status — exportedOn timestamp is sufficient
- Export allowed only when status is COMPLETED or REVIEWED
- PDF structure: header (name, description, metadata) + sections in sortOrder
- Section with no content → "Not mentioned in the provided information (MOM)"
- Use @SkipTransform() decorator on export endpoint to bypass TransformResponseInterceptor

## TransformResponseInterceptor
- Registered via APP_INTERCEPTOR in AppModule (not app.useGlobalInterceptors)
- Uses Reflector for @SkipTransform() decorator support
- Returns EMPTY when response already sent (headersSent = true)
- @SkipTransform() decorator skips wrapping for raw response endpoints (PDF export)

## Async Generation Flow (SQS)
API layer responsibilities:
1. Validate request
2. Save MainGeneratedSpec to DB
3. Create GeneratedSpec with status PROCESSING
4. Set pendingVersion on MainGeneratedSpec
5. Upload MOM to S3
6. Push job to SQS
7. Return immediately — { id: mainSpecId, versionId: generatedSpecId }

Worker layer responsibilities:
1. Fetch spec + template sections from DB
2. Fetch MOM from S3
3. Call OpenAI (one call, JSON response)
4. Parse response — sections + risks
5. In @Transactional():
   a. Save GeneratedSpecSections
   b. Save SpecRisks with priority
   c. Update GeneratedSpec status → COMPLETED + AI metadata
   d. Deactivate previous versions (isActive = false)
   e. Update MainGeneratedSpec.currentVersion pointer
   f. Clear MainGeneratedSpec.pendingVersion (set to null)
6. Delete SQS message — only after transaction commits
On failure (outside transaction):
- Update GeneratedSpec status → FAILED
- Clear pendingVersion on MainGeneratedSpec
- Do NOT update currentVersion
- Do NOT delete SQS message — let SQS retry

## Regenerate Flow
1. Fetch MainGeneratedSpec — verify ownership
2. Create new GeneratedSpec (PROCESSING) with next version number
3. Set pendingVersion = new spec — do NOT update currentVersion
4. Upload new MOM to S3
5. Push to SQS
6. Return { id: mainSpecId, versionId: newGeneratedSpecId }
- Template language used automatically — no language selection by user
- Template always uses mainTemplate.currentVersion at time of regeneration

## SQS Job Payload
interface GenerationJobPayload {
  generatedSpecId: string;
  mainSpecId: string;
  userId: string;
  momS3Key: string;
  momS3Bucket: string;
}

## Architecture Rules
- Never call OpenAI from API layer (HTTP request lifecycle)
- OpenAI is only called from the SQS worker process
- Never update currentVersion before COMPLETED status
- Never update currentVersion on FAILED status
- API returns immediately after pushing to SQS
- Spec language is determined by template language — no user language selection

## Soft Delete Rules
- Never hard delete any record
- Soft delete only via deletedOn + deletedBy
- Always filter deletedOn: IsNull() on active record queries

## Database Schema Decisions
- MainGeneratedSpec.currentVersion — pointer to latest COMPLETED GeneratedSpec
- MainGeneratedSpec.pendingVersion — pointer to in-progress generation
- MainTemplate.currentVersion — pointer to latest active Template version
- section_type table DROPPED — template_section.description serves as AI guidance
- Language.code used by frontend (EN/TH) — backend resolves to id
- SpecStatus.code used by app — backend resolves to id
- RiskType.code used by AI response — backend resolves to id
- Language locked per GeneratedSpec version
- Spec content is read-only after generation
- Regenerate creates new GeneratedSpec version, never mutates existing
- MOM stored in S3 — store s3Key + s3Bucket, never full URLs
- mom_input_type tracks TEXT vs FILE for analytics — always upload to S3
- AI metadata stored per GeneratedSpec version
- spec_risk.priority: HIGH | MEDIUM / LOW — AI-determined
- main_generated_spec.description: optional, user-provided, editable after creation
- exported_on: timestamp on generated_spec — no EXPORTED status needed
- AuditLog: no FK on entity_id — survives entity deletion
- pending_version_id on main_generated_spec — regeneration indicator

## Indexes (already in schema.sql, do not re-create)
- user(email)
- audit_log(entity, entity_id)
- audit_log(changed_by)
- main_template(user_id, deleted_on)
- main_template(language_id)
- template(main_template_id, version)
- template_section(template_id, sort_order)
- main_generated_spec(user_id, deleted_on)
- main_generated_spec(pending_version_id) WHERE pending_version_id IS NOT NULL
- generated_spec(main_spec_id, version)
- generated_spec(status_id)
- generated_spec_section(spec_id, sort_order)
- spec_risk(spec_id)
- spec_risk(section_id)

## Local Dev Stack
- spec-app-pg:         PostgreSQL 16 (localhost:5432)
- spec-app-redis:      Redis 7 (localhost:6379) — reserved, not wired yet
- spec-app-sonarqube:  SonarQube community (localhost:9000)
- SQS: real AWS dev queue (no localstack)
- Docker network: spec-app-network

## ECS Deployment
Single Docker image, two ECS task definitions:
- spec-app-api-task    → CMD: node dist/main.js (port 8000)
- spec-app-worker-task → CMD: node dist/worker.main.js (no port)

GitHub Actions (OIDC — no long-lived keys):
- Triggers on push to main
- Builds image from monorepo root Dockerfile
- Pushes to ECR with git SHA tag + latest tag
- Deploys both API and Worker ECS services

Dockerfile at monorepo root:
- Builds packages/schemas first
- Then builds backend
- Production stage copies only dist + node_modules

## AWS Infrastructure (see .cursorrules.local for resource IDs)
- Region: ap-southeast-1
- VPC with 2 public + 2 private subnets
- No NAT Gateway (cost saving)
- No ALB (cost saving, pre-prod)
- ECS Fargate in public subnets with public IP enabled
- RDS PostgreSQL in private subnets (no public access)
- S3 private bucket for MOM storage
- SQS standard queue for generation jobs
- Secrets Manager for all app secrets
- ECR for Docker images
- CloudWatch Logs for ECS task logs (/ecs/spec-app-api, /ecs/spec-app-worker)

## IAM Roles
- Execution role: pulls ECR images + reads Secrets Manager + writes CloudWatch logs
- Task role: SQS (send/receive/delete) + S3 (put/get/delete) — no other permissions
- GitHub Actions role: ECR push + ECS deploy via OIDC (no long-lived keys)

## What NOT To Do
- Do NOT use synchronize: true
- Do NOT use eager loading on any relation
- Do NOT return raw entities from controllers
- Do NOT call AWS SDK for secrets at bootstrap
- Do NOT add @Exclude() decorators
- Do NOT hard delete any record
- Do NOT update currentVersion on FAILED status
- Do NOT call OpenAI from HTTP request lifecycle
- Do NOT use BullMQ (using SQS directly)
- Do NOT use localstack (use real AWS dev SQS queue)
- Do NOT add NAT Gateway or ALB to infra
- Do NOT use Aurora Serverless (not in current scope)
- Do NOT store full S3 URLs (store key + bucket only)
- Do NOT add chat AI interface (system is workflow-based only)
- Do NOT repeat audit columns on individual entities (use BaseEntity)
- Do NOT repeat isActive on individual entities (use BaseEntity)
- Do NOT add id column on individual entities (use BaseEntity)
- Do NOT create section-type entity or reference section_type table
- Do NOT add username field to user entity (email only)
- Do NOT use class-based DTOs with static from() — use Zod inferred types
- Do NOT share repositories across modules
- Do NOT export repositories from feature modules
- Do NOT put business logic in repositories
- Do NOT resolve status/type codes to IDs inside repositories
- Do NOT add EXPORTED as a spec status — use exported_on timestamp instead
- Do NOT pass EntityManager to repository methods — use @Transactional() decorator
- Do NOT delete SQS message on failure — let SQS retry
- Do NOT update currentVersion inside transaction before all saves complete
- Do NOT require AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID / AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY — make optional
- Do NOT use getOrThrow for optional AWS credential config values
- Do NOT add PENDING status — specs start with PROCESSING
- Do NOT allow language selection on spec create or regenerate — use template language
- Do NOT update currentVersion before worker completes successfully
- Do NOT clear pendingVersion before worker completes or fails
- Do NOT register TransformResponseInterceptor via app.useGlobalInterceptors
  — use APP_INTERCEPTOR provider in AppModule for Reflector injection support
- Do NOT commit AWS account IDs, ARNs, or resource names — use .cursorrules.local
